--TEST--
`Core\Xml\Node::source` is the way out of a walk: what it writes parses to the tree that wrote it, and writing that again answers the same text — `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`, `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Xml\Node::source
// The tree half materialises a document and this is how one leaves again,
// without replaying the walk into a `Core\Xml\Writer` a call at a time. Two
// things are visible in what comes out: an empty element carries an end tag
// rather than being written `<empty/>`, and no `<?xml …?>` declaration is
// written back, because a parse leaves none behind to write.
Core\Xml\Node $doc = Core\Xml::parse("<?xml version=\"1.0\"?><!--an order--><order id=\"A&amp;B\"><line n=\"2\">2 &lt; 3</line><empty/><?render mode=compact?></order>");
tainted string $written = $doc->source();
echo $written, "\n";

// Writing what was just written answers the same text, so the round trip is a
// fixed point rather than a document that drifts each time it is read back.
tainted string $again = Core\Xml::parse($written)->source();
if ($again == $written) {
    echo "fixed point\n";
} else {
    echo "drifted: ", $again, "\n";
}

// And the other shape agrees about it: the stream reads back the nodes the
// tree held, in the order the tree held them.
Core\Xml\Reader $reader = Core\Xml::reader($written);
while (true) {
    ?Core\Xml\Node $node = $reader->read();
    if ($node != null) {
        echo "  ", $reader->depth(), " \"", $node->name(), "\" \"", $node->text(), "\"\n";
    } else {
        break;
    }
}
--EXPECT--
<!--an order--><order id="A&amp;B"><line n="2">2 &lt; 3</line><empty></empty><?render mode=compact?></order>
fixed point
  0 "" "an order"
  0 "order" ""
  1 "line" ""
  2 "" "2 < 3"
  1 "empty" ""
  1 "render" "mode=compact"
